id: pm-9sui7t
title: "Both token gates are structurally unable to fail: one measures only help payloads, the other measures command output against a three-item fixture, so no check anywhere compares real output to the ceiling the contract declares"
description: "The project declares a per-command output token ceiling in the contracts table and runs two gates named after token cost, and neither gate can observe a command exceeding its ceiling. Both were shipped by closed work that is otherwise correct; the defect is what they measure, not whether they exist.\n\nGate one, scripts/release/token-budget-gate.mjs, invoked as quality:token-budget and inside quality:static.\n\n  - seedFixture (:51) creates exactly three items in an isolated temporary PM_PATH.\n  - commandCorpus (:127) measures 17 surfaces against that three-item workspace.\n  - The oracle is scripts/release/token-budgets.json, whose numbers are recorded observations of that fixture, not the contract-declared ceilings. list-default is budgeted at 649 bytes because three items produce 649 bytes.\n  - The comparison at :230 is measurement.bytes against budget.max_bytes. default_max_estimated_tokens is never read. The contract ceiling is not this gate's oracle in any form.\n  - The corpus omits every surface that actually overruns: activity, deps, graph, duplicates, events, and bare health are all absent.\n  - It runs in nightly.yml:92 and release.yml:107 through quality:static. It does not run in ci.yml, so no pull request is gated on command output size.\n\nMeasured consequence, same command, same build, different workspace scale:\n\n  pm list on the 3-item fixture      649 bytes    (gate budget: 649, passes)\n  pm list on this repository      56,937 bytes    (87.7x the gate budget, unobserved)\n\nThe gate reports \"Token budget gate passed (17 surfaces checked)\" on a machine where pm activity emits 1,591,219 estimated tokens against a declared 4,000. It is structurally incapable of failing for the reason it exists, because output size on this surface is a function of workspace scale and the fixture has no scale.\n\nGate two, scripts/measure-agent-token-surface.mjs against scripts/agent-token-surface-baseline.json, invoked as quality:token-surface and chained into quality:context-eval, which does run on every CI pull request.\n\n  - The committed baseline contains root_help, per_command_total, full_help_surface, three contracts payload sizes, mcp_tools_list, and a required_commands array that is a list of command names.\n  - Every measured value is a help, contracts, or MCP registration payload. Not one is the output of a command doing work.\n\nSo the surface that is gated on pull requests measures only discovery cost, and the surface that measures command output is both fixture-bound and off the pull-request path. Between them there is no point in the pipeline where a command's real output is compared against the ceiling its contract declares.\n\nContract-declared ceilings are therefore decorative on both sides: not enforced at runtime, and not the oracle of either gate. That is why the 397.8x, 11.6x, 5.5x and 3.6x overruns measured on this workspace all shipped without a single failing check.\n\nThis is the same defect class as pm-lpqln4, where the only Bun coverage in the pipeline passes with an arbitrary token in the executable position: a gate whose passing carries no information. Recording the class explicitly matters more than either instance, because the remedy is a shared one -- a gate must be built with a negative control that proves it fails when the property it guards is violated.\n\nRelationship to neighbours. pm-5t33or is the forward-looking feature that makes ceilings binding at runtime and its acceptance criteria already require extending the corpus gate; this item is the measured present-tense defect that motivates it and supplies the negative control that criterion needs. pm-cu1i and pm-dpqa3h are the closed items that shipped these two gates, and neither is wrong about what it delivered. pm-a22j is the original baseline measurement. The individual overruns each have their own owner and are not re-filed here."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 1
